on checks and documents a year by hand, a task that consumed an estimated 13 billion keystrokes annually.\nAnything that compresses turnaround time and reduces manual handling on that intake flows directly into how fast and how accurately collectors can post payments.\n\nThe scale JPMorgan describes underscores why automation here matters. The bank’s lockbox operators previously processed roughly 480 million checks and documents a year by hand, a task that consumed an estimated 13 billion keystrokes annually. JPMorgan says those tasks are now largely automated, with processing accuracy reported at 99.999 percent or better. The robots are engineered to recognize more than 4,000 envelope and document permutations, the kind of layout variability that humans navigate easily but that has historically been difficult to encode for machines.\n\nThe robotics layer sits on top of earlier work. JPMorgan rebuilt its underlying lockbox processing platform in 2020 using AI for data extraction, quality validation, and review, and has since integrated large language models to handle complex review tasks. Operators now work alongside an AI assistant that surfaces real-time information on throughput, exceptions, and turnaround, letting staff concentrate on judgment calls rather than data entry. The bank frames the effort as part of a broader $19.8 billion annual technology investment.\n\nThe backdrop is a paper check problem that refuses to disappear. An Association for Financial Professionals survey found the share of organizations using checks rose from 75 percent in 2023 to 91 percent in 2024, even as 63 percent reported experiencing check fraud or attempted fraud. Vendors continue to bet on the format’s persistence, including Deluxe, which paid $960 million for First American Payments in 2022 to expand its own lockbox and receivables business.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKraken Robotics Inc. (https://www.krakenrobotics.com/wp-c